# Cloudflare Pages redirects. Mirrors the [[redirects]] blocks in netlify.toml,
# which stays untouched for as long as Netlify is still serving cerostudio.co.
#
# Format: <from> <to> <status>. First match wins, so order matters.
#
# Cloudflare Pages already strips .html and trailing slashes on its own, with a
# 308. The rules below exist to pin that behaviour to the 301 netlify.toml
# declares, rather than leaving the canonical form to a platform default that
# could change under us. They are loop-safe either way: each target is an
# extensionless URL that serves a 200 and is matched by no rule here.

# Canonical URL form: the extensionless one, which is what canonical tags,
# og:url and sitemap.xml all declare. Listed one by one on purpose, exactly as
# in netlify.toml. A wildcard would also catch google7aa936a6fd430f8b.html.
/index.html / 301
/portfolio.html /portfolio 301
/founder.html /founder 301
/co-founder.html /co-founder 301

# NOT redirected here, on purpose:
#
# google7aa936a6fd430f8b.html - the Search Console verification file. Netlify
# left it at its exact URL. Cloudflare Pages strips the .html unconditionally
# and a _redirects rule cannot stop it: Pages supports no 200-status rewrite,
# so there is no way to serve that path without a redirect. The 308 it issues
# stays on the same host and the token is served intact, which Search Console
# accepts, so verification holds. Moving that property to DNS TXT verification
# once the nameservers are on Cloudflare removes the dependency entirely.
#
# www.cerostudio.co -> cerostudio.co - netlify.toml handles this with a
# force'd host redirect. Cloudflare Pages _redirects cannot match on hostname
# (domain-level redirects are unsupported), so this one does not survive as a
# file. It has to be recreated in the dashboard as a Redirect Rule on the zone
# before cerostudio.co cuts over, or the apex and www both answer and split
# the canonical host.
